explanation:
The underlying multiset is the bind (concatenation) of the multisets of t a over a ∈ s. That is exactly the multiset sum of the members of the family. It has no duplicates because:
- each t a has no duplicates;
- s has no duplicates;
- the t a are pairwise disjoint on s.

So its elements are exactly the b with b ∈ t a for some a ∈ s, which is the union. Because the sets are disjoint, the union is computed without needing decidable equality on β.

The setting is fully general: arbitrary types α and β, and the only hypothesis is the pairwise disjointness the construction needs. It agrees with `Finset.biUnion` whenever both are defined. The docstring says the same.
